  "founding_year": 2004,
  "history": "Founded in Menlo Park in 2004 by Vinod Khosla, a co-founder of Sun Microsystems and a general partner at Kleiner Perkins from 1986. The firm invested only Khosla's own capital through Khosla Ventures I and II and took no outside limited partners until 2009, when Khosla Ventures III and the first Seed fund closed together. Its first decade was dominated by cleantech \u2014 biofuels, solar, batteries and materials, a book that produced Amyris, LanzaTech, Ausra, KiOR and View \u2014 and the practice has since widened into consumer, enterprise, fintech, health and deep tech while keeping the same posture toward technical risk. The firm ran a dedicated seed programme from 2009 (Seed through Seed G), added Opportunity funds for growth in 2021, and sponsored four blank-check companies in 2021 of which one, KVSA II, completed a merger with Nextdoor.",
  "investment_concept": "Backs technical founders taking science and engineering risk that most investors will not price, on the explicit view that a small number of improbable outcomes carries a portfolio. The firm runs three vehicles against one thesis: a seed fund for the earliest and least legible companies, a main fund that follows them and enters new ones, and Opportunity funds that concentrate into the winners. It states its own preference for what it calls black-swan bets, and its book shows it \u2014 fusion, hypersonics, in-space manufacturing, synthetic biology and the first venture cheque into OpenAI sit alongside DoorDash, Instacart, Square and Affirm.",
